<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Siege</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siege of Basra (1775–1776)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1775">1775</a>–<a href="/years/1776">1776</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> (1775–1776) was the capture of the Ottoman-held port city of Basra by the forces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zand-dynasty">Zand Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan</a>. The Zands expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> from Basra in 1776. The city was later lost again after Karim Khan's death.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1774 an undeclared war began between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zand-dynasty">Zand dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> over trade and border skirmishes between Khosrow Bozorgi of Ardalan and the Ottoman pasha of Baban. In April 1774 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-kab">Banu Ka'b</a> tribesmen had also attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> and burned parts of the city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan Zand</a> sent a standing army under his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadeq-khan-zand">Sadeq Khan Zand</a> to take the port, which was held by a local governor subordinate to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. On 16 March 1775 Sadeq was at Sowayb with about 30,000 troops.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Sadeq took personal command on 6 April 1775 and began the siege with heavy bombardment and assaults on the walls. He was aided by Shaykh Naser of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bushehr">Bushehr</a>, a Zand vassal, with 1,500 men and a fleet, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-kab">Banu Ka'b</a> on land. A British squadron under Henry Moore tried to cut off Banu Ka'b help with a chain across the water, but Shaykh Naser broke through it, and Moore, left without support, withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> in July. The al-Jalili family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> and Ömer Pasha of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> gave the garrison little or no help.</p><p>At the request of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> sent an armada, which entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> in October 1775 and lifted the defenders' morale, but supplies ran out and the Omanis withdrew in early 1776. As conditions in the city worsened, some inhabitants left and were fed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zand-dynasty">Zands</a>. The garrison commander, Soleyman Aqa, opened talks on 16 April and surrendered on 20 April 1776; Sadeq entered the next day.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Sadeq sent the city's political and financial leaders, Soleyman Aqa among them, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> and left a garrison of 6,000. Coins were struck in the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan</a> and of the twelve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a>, and Shia Friday sermons were introduced. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-kab">Banu Ka'b</a> received the fields of Hindiyan for an annual tax of 1,000 tumans. The damage to the city and the British withdrawal meant that the conquest brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zand-dynasty">Zands</a> little advantage in trade. The Zand administration lasted until Karim Khan's death in 1779. The borders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-zuhab">Treaty of Zuhab</a> were then affirmed again, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> never recovered from the destruction, and Persian trade routes moved north.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Siege of Basra (1775–1776)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
